\caption(An actual delete-one statistic and its pseudo-value on three observations.)
\alttext(The full mean, deletion of value four and algebraic pseudo-value four.)

> Derive the delete-one summary

Let theta be the full-sample statistic and theta_(i) the statistic with index i
removed, in original retained-value order. Write their average as theta_bar. The
bias estimate, corrected statistic and pseudo-values are

$$
\widehat b=(n-1)(\bar\theta-\hat\theta),\qquad
\hat\theta_J=\hat\theta-\widehat b,\qquad
p_i=n\hat\theta-(n-1)\hat\theta_{(i)}.
$$
\alttext(The bias is the scaled average deletion drift; the corrected statistic equals the mean pseudo-value in exact arithmetic.)

The average pseudo-value equals the corrected statistic by algebra. For a
regular estimator with an expectation expansion theta+c/n plus a sufficiently
regular remainder, the n versus n−1 comparison estimates the leading c/n bias.
That argument is an assumption about the estimator and sampling model, not a
universal guarantee that every corrected statistic is unbiased.

The delete-one variance estimate has equivalent replicate and pseudo-value
forms:

$$
\widehat{SE}_J^2=\frac{n-1}{n}\sum_i(\hat\theta_{(i)}-\bar\theta)^2
=\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\sum_i(p_i-\bar p)^2.
$$
\alttext(The replicate variance inflation and pseudo-value variance formulas are exactly equivalent algebraically.)

For the mean, theta_(i)=(n*mean−x_i)/(n−1), the deletion average is the full
mean, and p_i=x_i. Hence bias is zero and SE squared is s squared divided by n.
These are exact-arithmetic identities; tiny floating residuals in computed bias
are not scientific bias. Pseudo-values for general statistics are dependent
transforms; the variance formula is not proof of independent observations or a
confidence interval.

> Delete more, and enumerate a case small enough to inspect

A delete-d subset retains m=n−d observations. Averaging over all C(n,d) distinct
deletion sets gives the conditional subset-statistic variance v_sub. The
implemented delete-d estimate is

$$
\widehat V_d=\frac{n-d}{d}\frac{1}{M}\sum_{S:|S|=d}
(\hat\theta_{(-S)}-\bar\theta_d)^2,
\qquad M=\binom nd.
$$
\alttext(The complete delete-d variance is the population variance of subset statistics multiplied by retained count over deleted count.)

For the mean of a uniformly selected retained subset, finite-population sampling
gives v_sub=d*s squared/(m*n). Multiplying by m/d gives s squared/n for every
valid deletion count. One derivation uses inclusion indicators: each has mean
m/n and distinct indicators have covariance −m*(n−m)/(n squared*(n−1));
substituting these into the variance of the retained mean yields the expression
above.

The next program enumerates all six delete-two sets on [1,2,4,9]. Each table row
is paired with its actual callback, and the complete delete-two mean SE agrees
with the delete-one identity. Enumeration is over indices, so equal observed
values would still represent different deletion sets.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jackknife_exact.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/jackknife_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/bootstrap.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <algorithm>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const std::vector<double> x{1, 2, 4, 9};
    const auto f =
        dm::stats::jackknife_statistic(dm::stats::JackknifeStatistic::Mean);
    const auto t = dm::stats::jackknife_delete_d_trace(x, f, {2, 6, 7});
    std::print("| actual subset | first deleted index | second deleted index | retained mean |\n| ---: | " "---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<double> index;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.replicates.size(); ++i) {
        index.push_back(double(i));
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.8f} |\n", i, t.deletion_sets[i][0],
                   t.deletion_sets[i][1], t.replicates[i]);
    }
    std::print("Complete {}, subsets {}, callbacks {}, subset-statistic variance {:.8f}, delete-two SE " "{:.8f}, delete-one SE {:.8f}.\n",
               t.result.complete, t.result.num_subsets, t.evaluations,
               t.replicate_population_variance, t.result.standard_error,
               dm::stats::jackknife(x, f).standard_error);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(index, t.replicates, "actual retained means", {35, 95, 210}, 5).abline(t.result.estimate, 0, {210, 55, 40}, 1.5).title("All six actual retained-two means").x_label("lexicographic deletion-set index").y_label("retained subset mean").ylim(0, 8).save_svg("build/jackknife_exact.svg");
}
```
