> Preserve the iris variance correction and mean identity

The canonical iris DataFrame supplies all 150 Sepal.Length values in cm,
including ties and original row order. The observations are pooled across the
three species, so this is a resampling diagnostic on the fixed empirical sample,
not a demonstrated sampling design for a flower population.

Let Q be the sum of squared deviations around the full mean. The plug-in
variance is Q/n; deleting observation i leaves

$$
Q_{(-i)}=Q-\frac{n}{n-1}(x_i-\bar x)^2,\qquad
\hat\sigma^2_{J}=\frac{Q}{n-1}.
$$
\alttext(Deleting an observation subtracts its adjusted squared deviation, and jackknife correction of the plug-in variance gives the sample variance.)

Summing the deletion expression gives mean deleted plug-in variance
Q*(n−2)/((n−1)*(n−1)). Substitution into the bias formula yields corrected
Q/(n−1). For the displayed influence figure we retain the original choice of
sample variance: the leave-one-out values divide Q_(-i) by n−2. This is a
downward-opening quadratic in the deleted value. Removing an extreme value
lowers the remaining variance; deleting a central value raises it slightly. The
original report's description of a U-shape had the curvature reversed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jackknife_iris.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/jackknife_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/bootstrap.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <algorithm>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
    const auto &x = iris.get_column("Sepal.Length").values();
    const auto mean =
        dm::stats::jackknife_statistic(dm::stats::JackknifeStatistic::Mean);
    const auto population =
        dm::stats::jackknife_statistic(dm::stats::JackknifeStatistic::PopulationVariance);
    const auto sample =
        dm::stats::jackknife_statistic(dm::stats::JackknifeStatistic::SampleVariance);
    const auto jv = dm::stats::jackknife_checked(x, population);
    const auto jm = dm::stats::jackknife_checked(x, mean);
    const auto jvar = dm::stats::jackknife_checked(x, sample);
    std::print("| statistic | full estimate | estimated bias | corrected estimate | jackknife SE | " "algebraic target |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::print("| plug-in variance | {:.12f} | {:.8e} | {:.12f} | {:.10f} | {:.12f} |\n",
               jv.estimate, jv.bias, jv.bias_corrected, jv.standard_error,
               sample(x));
    std::print("| mean | {:.12f} | {:.8e} | {:.12f} | {:.10f} | {:.10f} |\n",
               jm.estimate, jm.bias, jm.bias_corrected, jm.standard_error,
               std::sqrt(sample(x) / x.size()));
    std::print("Rows {}, all original-order leave-one-out sample variances {}.\n",
               iris.nrows(), jvar.leave_one_out.size());
    dm::stats::jackknife_influence_plot(x, jvar).title("Iris: actual sample variance after deleting each flower").x_label("deleted Sepal.Length (cm)").y_label("leave-one-out sample variance (cm squared)").ylim(.65, .71).save_svg("build/jackknife_iris.svg");
}
