> Implementation, ownership and statistical scope

Delete-one requires n+1 statistic calls and O(n squared) value copying, plus
callback costs. It owns n actual leave-one-out values and n pseudo-values, with
O(n) working/output storage. The checked variant admits the complete n inputs
and2n returned doubles before callbacks/copies. The legacy call disables only
optional ceilings; finite-value and shape checks still apply.

Delete-d computes the binomial count by an exact capped integer recurrence. It
enumerates lexicographic deletion indices if the count fits the positive budget,
otherwise samples exactly the budgeted number of sets using a local MT19937-64
engine and the retained partial Fisher-Yates permutation policy. Kept
observations retain original order. The ordinary function streams centred
mean/variance without retaining subset histories: O(n+d) workspace and O(B*(n+d
log d)) copying/selection plus B+1 callback costs. The trace retains B*(d+1)
actual deletion-index/replicate entries, admitted completely before
callbacks/copies. There is no RNG replay or repeated-prefix fit.

Default optional limits are one million input elements and one million retained
scalar entries; zero disables a cap. These exclude borrowed sample, scalar
summaries, kept/permutation work vectors, capacities and byte/peak RAM. Indices
and doubles both count as scalar entries, not equal-size physical memory units.
Graphics have a separate complete point quota and validate displayed counts and
finite values, not edited-history provenance. All returned vectors are owned; no
input/callback references or shared mutable state are retained. Callback
exceptions propagate and already-executed side effects persist.

Both methods validate finite n>=2 samples and nonempty callbacks; delete-d
requires1<=d<n and a positive subset budget. Every callback result must be
finite. Wide centred summaries, robust norms and checked finite-double outputs
avoid avoidable uncentred overflow, including constant maximal-double callbacks;
unrepresentable summaries/pseudo-values throw. Wider arithmetic is platform
dependent and does not recover information already lost by a callback. Native
statistic bridges delegate mean, median and sample SD to existing vector APIs.
The original result/options field layouts are retained; stable arithmetic can
change final digits. Invalid zero budgets now throw instead of dividing by zero
and masking the resulting NaN as a zero standard error.

The
[Shao and Wu general-theory reference|https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1176347263] is
Annals of Statistics17(3),1176–1197(1989). Its
[JSTOR publication record|https://www.jstor.org/stable/2241717] confirms the
bibliographic entry. The identities and finite conditional calculations above
are derived explicitly; this report does not assert an asymptotic consistency
theorem for this rounded iris sample. Smoothness, sampling design, dependence,
ties and deletion-size choices matter. The mean and variance identities pass;
the median examples expose limits and sensitivity rather than establish a
universal delete-d repair.

> Verification

Twenty selected native cases pass normally and with AddressSanitizer and
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, including all deletion counts on1089 ternary
samples, 4923 complete delete-d mean fits, actual callback order/sets, mean and
plug-in variance identities, enumeration boundaries, huge binomial admission,
seeded sampling, range/constant controls, caps, ownership and native graphics.
Existing bootstrap, U/V-statistic and permutation cases pass within this
selected suite. Independent rational checks cover2438 runs,62058 actual delete-d
sets,5266 actual leave-one-out values and5266 pseudo-values. They recompute
subset statistics, mode/count decisions, bias correction and variance scaling
from the actual records. The4000 actual bootstrap replicate values independently
reproduce its reported standard deviation; this does not claim a separate
bootstrap RNG audit.

R verifies all150 Sepal.Length inputs against its canonical iris dataset, all600
leave-one-out statistic values and all3000 actual retained-130 medians for the
original settings, as well as their summary statistics. A frozen-code comparison
preserves every one of11972 actual deletion sets over476 finite controls, with
SE differences below6.7e-16. The original iris median callback and delete-d loop
also reproduce the fresh original-settings result. All51 module/helper
definitions are documented, thirteen public examples compile and execute, and
four representative clangd hovers pass. These are bounded method and experiment
checks, not a certificate of population coverage or of the historical report
corpus.
